Z||000002||hugh||03-04-2001||07:29 PM||||I'm from the 50's.Worked on my cars used vaseline on my hair(like most everybody else).I guess I'm a greaser like John Travolta.I'm surely not a ricer.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-04-2001||07:53 PM||||I remember being called a "greaser" for working on cars a lot. Also called a gearhead.<BR>As for the term "ricer" I thought that came from persons who decorated the outside of a car tastelessly. I always thought the term came from wedding cars. All decorated with streamers and the likes in bright colors. (ricer being related to the throwing of the rice) I first heard the term back in the late 70's/ early 80's when the fad was to put bright odd colored accessories on the cars. Example: flourecent green wipers, hubcaps, and antenna on a dark blue car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||03-04-2001||10:14 PM||||I think a far more accurate name for people who spend theirmodification budget on non-functional wannabe "performance" stuff would be "posers".  Certainly import owners do not have the market cornered on 4" exhaust tips on 1" pipe, or double wiper blades or any one of the myriad of products that keeps Pep Boys cash registers ringing.<P>The term "riceboy" is about as played out as the phrase "Talk to the hand" or "you go, girl". Time to leave the past behind, kids.||||reg||1
